proof testing_a_test = cleanup;
proof a_value_tested_twice = all;

// Never `copy_const`, which is the matcher that would answer in one step.
// `inv(share { X })` reads naturality backwards to put the second `push` in a
// frame, and the movement laws take the frame away again — which is the
// derivation `applier::tests::copy_const_is_derivable_from_copy_nat` runs, said
// in the tactic language.
proof copying_a_constant =
    must(once(inv(share { push 9 })));
    must(at(0, sink));
    must(at(0, flatten));

// One annihilation and two counits, which is the whole content of the claim
// that this is a lemma rather than an axiom. `applier::tests::\
// vacuous_is_derivable_from_annihilate_and_counit` runs the same derivation
// over an opaque `X`; here it is a concrete one, stated in the language.
proof discarded_work_on_copies = cleanup;

// The same proof as `testing_a_test`. What differs is the right-hand side,
// which names a sentence rather than spelling it out — so this one closes up to
// inlining where that one closes exactly.
proof testing_a_test_by_name = cleanup;

// Neither side is the normal form, so neither can be derived from the other by
// rewriting one of them. `normalize` runs the tactic on both and meets, and the
// derivation is the left's steps followed by the right's inverted.
proof two_spellings_of_one_test = normalize(cleanup);

// Congruence, said out loud: the goal is cut into one sub-goal per arm, the
// `then` arms get the proof above, and the `else` arms are claimed equal
// already — which `descend` checks rather than assumes.
//
// Nothing here is inferred. There is no strategy that would have decomposed on
// its own initiative, because decomposition can turn a provable goal into an
// unprovable one: `push 1 ; drop` and `push 2 ; drop` are equal, and peeling
// the shared `drop` leaves a false goal behind. Where to cut is the author's
// call, and this is where it is written down.
proof a_test_inside_an_arm = descend(then: normalize(cleanup));

// Narrow, then decompose. `descend` comes last because it is the one thing that
// cannot be a move: a branch forks into two sub-goals and a sequence has one
// place to put what follows, so what discharges each arm goes inside it.
proof a_test_inside_an_arm_with_a_prefix =
    peel ; descend(then: normalize(cleanup));

// One firing of `bool_result_copied`, which is the eight steps above placed by
// a matcher rather than aimed one at a time. `must` because the point is that
// something *finds* it: writing the eight out by hand was always possible.
proof the_guard_a_split_leaves = must(once(bool_result_copied));

// `inv(roll_cycle)` to put a cycle of rolls in, then the law itself. Placed by
// one matcher reading the frame, which is the whole of what was missing.
proof taking_a_frame_off = must(once(unframe));

// Two evaluations and a third, which is all it ever needed to be.
proof comparing_two_built_tuples = values;
